>> >Nancy Lebovitz writes:
>> 
>> I want to distinguish at least two levels of genetic design--eliminating
>> well-defined flaws and designing in added capabilities. (I split the
>> latter into three levels--adding capabilities that some people have,
>> adding capabilities known in other species (probably mostly animals,
>> but who knows?), and adding capabilities that have never been seen
>> before.)
>
>An interesting starting point might be level zero: selecting capabilities
>that are in the genetic heritage of the individual. I'd like to be able to
>pick and choose between some of the traits that my offspring now have only
>a chance of inheriting. For example, my SO and I both carry genes for red
>hair, which we would like to propagate, and we both have close relatives
>with diabetes, which we would NOT like to pass on.   
>
Actually, I think it's level 1 1/2--eliminating well-defined flaws
is part of level one and selecting for the best traits of the
parents is proto-level two. 

This does inspire me to split level 2.c into two parts--amplifying
existing human abilities (for instance, creating a person who can
run a two minute mile) and (much more difficult) designing in
abilities that have never been seen in the human race. 

If I'm not mistaken, what you describe is the degree of genetic
manipulation used in Heinlein's _Beyond This Horizon_. (The technology
existed for more ambitious manipulation, but the society (government?)
had decided that it had worked so badly for a previous dictatorship
that they'd given it up.)

Do you know how the genes for red hair work? I have a vague impression
that it's the result of having genes for both brown and blond hair
rather than particular genes.

I might as well mention that one of the reasons I'm a little edgy
about genetic manipulation (though mostly optimistic about it) is
that I have a trait which I've seen described as worth eliminating
but which I'm not convinced is all that bad.

I'm short. However, I'm not paranoid.....I've seen both RAWilson
and Esfandiary (_Up-wingers_ and a couple of other futurist books)
describe tallness as somthing that it's obvious to choose for.

Nancy Lebovitz 



