Nancy Lebovitz says
>I might as well mention that one of the reasons I'm a little edgy
>about genetic manipulation (though mostly optimistic about it) is
>that I have a trait which I've seen described as worth eliminating
>but which I'm not convinced is all that bad.
>
>I'm short. However, I'm not paranoid.....I've seen both RAWilson
>and Esfandiary (_Up-wingers_ and a couple of other futurist books)
>describe tallness as something that it's obvious to choose for.
>

This is already an issue causing controversy even before the availability of
methods for height trait selection.  Biotech companies have used recombinant
DNA techniques to make possible the economic production of human growth
hormone.  Human growth hormone has been used successfully to increase the
height of individuals who would have manifested congenital dwarfism.  Some
parents have pushed for the use of human growth hormone to increase the
height of children who without treatment would have projected heights
moderately below the norm.  Clinical studies are underway now to determine
if height can actually be increased in children whose natural height falls
within a ``normal'' range.

The articles I have read on this subject usually focus on the wishes of the
parents. The childrens wishes are less clear. Some appear unenthusiastic
about the treatment but others apparently welcome it.  The medical ethicists
quoted in the articles uniformly oppose the treatment of children whose
height falls in the ``normal'' range.  The prototypical libertarian response
would emphasize the freedom and autonomy of the child in making decisions
about its bodily form.  I consider the controversy worth monitoring because
it is a harbinger of future societal responses to physical and mental
modifications.  Currently ethicists wish to maintain a dichotomy between
amelioration and enhancement. This distinction will be difficult to
sustain.      Gregory Sullivan



