Vigdor Schreibman says
>Thank you for your interesting note, Greg.  I intend to get the two 
>volumes you recommend to see what the author's have to say.  I rather 
>liked the ideas expressed about nanotechnology when I first read a bit 
>about them some years ago, but I confess I know little about the two 
>works you list.

Your avowal in this message is admirable.  If you read the books with an
open mind then I think something of value will have emerged from our
exchange.

>As for the flawed "Limits to Growth," I couldn't agree more.  That work
>was an expresion of the overly prideful mistakes and premature publicity
>seeking of one celebrated MIT computer "genius," but note the two
>scientists who originated the work of the Club of Rome that led to that
>report (Hasan Ozbekhan, and Aleco Christakis, who personally informed me
>of that situation) (along with the Italian industrialist who put up the
>money), resigned from the project prior to publication of their
>misinformed book, for the very reasons you mention.  

The book I was critiquing was not ``Limits to Growth''. It was:

Donella Meadows, Dennis Meadows
and Jorgen Randers:             Beyond the Limits

This book is the sequel to ``Limits to Growth'' and it appeared in 1993.
This new book was lauded by many environmentalists. Below are some
blurbs:

``If you read only one book...make this it'' 
               Hunter Lovins, Rocky Mountain Institute

``The most important book you may ever read''
               Conservation Law Foundation

``No. 1 of the top 10 Environmental Books of the year.''
               Buzzworm, Earth Journal 1993

Despite the praise from environmentalists I found the book flawed as
indicated in my critique.  It is true that my criticism of the computer
model used in the new book also applies to the original book.  

I am not privy to the internal disagreements between the researchers that you
mention above but I do note that the new book ``Beyond the Limits'' is
dedicated to the Italian industrialist (Aurelio Peccei) that you mention.
If Peccei disavowed the authors work then they clearly did not reciprocate
with antagonism.

>The upshot is that
>your criticism of the entire environmental movement for the mistakes of one
>individual who was firmly rebuked by his own associates at the time, is so
>very unwarranted. 

My criticism of mainstream environmentalism is not based solely on my
critique of ``Beyond the Limits''.  Julian Simon's books, for example,
embody a more comprehensive critique which I will not reiterate here.  A
message from Dave Krieger gives the titles of some of Simon's books.  As
indicated at the end of my previous message if you are interested in a
debate between Simon and a mainstream environmentalist you can read the very
recent book.

Norman Myers and Julian Simon:  Scarcity or Abundance

